-- WHERE Clause : The WHERE clause is used to filter records based on a condition.
-- Syntax: SELECT column1, column2 FROM table_name WHERE condition;

-- Note : Think of it like Excel's Filter option: Show me only the rows where this condition is true.


-- SQL Operators : Operators are used to create conditions in the WHERE clause.

-- Comparion Operator  : 
-- | Operator | Meaning                  |
-- | -------- | ------------------------ |
-- | =        | Equal to                 | 
-- | <>       | Not equal to             | 
-- | >        | Greater than             |
-- | <        | Less than                |
-- | >=       | Greater than or equal to | 
-- | <=       | Less than or equal to    |

-- 1. Display all students whose Age is 20.
-- 2. Display all students whose City is not Mumbai.
-- 3. Display all students whose Marks are greater than 80.
-- 4. Display all students whose Marks are less than 60.
-- 5. Display all students whose Marks are greater than or equal to 75.
-- 6. Display all students whose Age is less than or equal to 20.


-- Logical Operators : used when we want to combine multiple conditions.

-- | Operator | Purpose                          
-- | -------- | ------------------------------------ |
-- | AND      | Both conditions must be true.        | 
-- | OR       | At least one condition must be true. | 
-- | NOT      | Reverses the condition.              |

-- 1.Display students who are from Mumbai AND have Marks greater than 80.
-- 2.Display students who are from Mumbai OR Pune.
-- 3.Display students who are NOT from Delhi.

